title: "Stolen Strata: Quantifying the Anthropogenic Erasure of Kashmir's Karewa Terraces and Its Threat to the Saffron Economy"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/STOLEN_STRATA"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/STOLEN_STRATA"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21766464"
    description: "Zenodo archive of Stolen Strata v1.0.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21766464"
abstract: >-
  A satellite remote-sensing study quantifying bare-earth conversion on
  Kashmir's karewa terraces — elevated, flat-topped remnants of a
  Pliocene-Pleistocene intermontane lake basin that underlie one of Kashmir's
  most economically important agricultural systems, the saffron cultivation
  belt of Pampore — a signal consistent with unregulated soil mining and
  unplanned urban expansion. This study
  delineates 201 karewa terraces (3,305.3 hectares) across the central
  Kashmir Valley using a Topographic Position Index and slope-threshold
  terrain analysis, then tracks their bare-earth land-cover fraction across
  four time slices (1994, 2005 [2001-2009 composite], 2015, 2025) using Landsat
  and Sentinel-2 composites (1994 and 2025 season-matched). Mean bare-earth fraction rose from 1.84% to 8.43%
  over the study period, concentrated almost entirely in the final decade;
  bare-earth-classified surface rose by a net 190.3 hectares, 67% of it
  within just 12.4% of terraces. A saffron-signature index, exploiting the
  crop's inverted phenology, identifies 14 likely saffron-cultivating
  terraces, 43% of which lie within 1 km of an already-degraded terrace
  despite no direct overlap. Mann-Whitney U tests indicate degraded
  terraces sit significantly closer to roads (p = 0.0116) and OSM building
  footprints (p = 0.0001) than intact terraces, an association consistent
  with accessibility-driven extraction. Threshold-sensitivity sweeps, a resolution-matched
  robustness check (resampling the 2025 Sentinel-2 composite to the 30m
  Landsat pixel size used in earlier years), and effect-size/multiple-
  comparison corrections show the headline pattern persists under the
  tested perturbations (other sensor differences and spatial dependence are
  not tested). Together these results indicate karewa degradation is recent,
  accelerating, spatially concentrated, and associated with infrastructure, with direct implications for how saffron-sector policy
  investment should be spatially targeted.
keywords:
  - karewa terraces
  - Kashmir
  - saffron
  - land degradation
  - remote sensing
  - multi-temporal change detection
  - unregulated mining
  - geomorphology
  - Google Earth Engine
  - spatial statistics
